chow with W6 or W9, but W9 gone, so only W6. That's a decent possibility.\n\nConsidering the T2-T5 block, I have four tiles in one suit consecutive. That's very strong for forming chows. I should preserve that. So I should discard a tile outside this block and outside my meld/pair.\n\nThus candidates for discard: W7, W8, B5, B6, B9. Among these, which is least useful? B9 is a terminal, and terminals are harder to use because they only have one side for chows (only 7-8-9 chow, need B7 and B8). I have no B7 or B8, and B7 only 2 left. So B9 is probably the worst. Also, B9 is a terminal, which might contribute to \"All Simples\" if discarded, but I have other terminals? W3 is not terminal (3), T2,3,4,5 are not terminals, B3 is not terminal. So my hand currently has no terminals except possibly B9. If I discard B9, my hand becomes all simples (no terminals or honors) which is a 2-point pattern. That's good. So discarding B9 moves me toward All Simples.\n\nWhat about other tiles: W7 and W8 are not terminals, but they are isolated. B5 and B6 are not terminals. Discarding B9 also removes a terminal, making the hand more flexible.\n\nAlso, from game history, opponents have discarded some terminals: W9, T9, B7. But no one has discarded B9 yet. Unshown B9 has 4 left, so it's safe? Possibly.\n\nBut I should also consider defensive play. Next player has a W9 kong and J1 pung, so maybe they are going for a hand with honors or terminals? But they discarded F4, B4, F1, W2, J3, which are mixed. Cross player discarded terminals and honors. Previous player discarded W8, J3, T9, J3. So many terminals and honors are out. Discarding B9 might be relatively safe.\n\nAlternatively, discarding W8: previous player discarded W8, so it might be safe. But W8 has 3 left, and W7-W8 together could become a chow if I draw W6. Keeping W8 might be useful if I draw W6. But I have only one W7 and one W8, so it's a weak connection. Compared to the T2-T5 block, the W7-W8 is less valuable.\n\nAlso, I have 