istory of the previous player is: Play W8, Play J3, Play T9, Play J3, Play F2, Play F3, Chi T7, Play T7, Play B7, Play T7, Chi T3, Play B5, Play T2, Play F3, Chi W7, Play B1, Play T9, Play F4, Play W1.\nThe followings are your valid actions:[Play W5; Play B3; Play W4; Play B6; Play T5; Play W3]\u3002", "reasoning": "", "output": "**Evaluation:**  \nMy hand has 9 tiles: three W3, one W4, one W5, two T5, two B3, two B6, plus one exposed Chow [T2,T3,T4].  \nPossible winning patterns: currently incomplete, but there\u2019s potential for All Simples (no terminals/honors in hand), maybe Half Flush (if focusing on W/T/B suits), or All Chows if I form more sequences.  \nKey observations:  \n- Many safe discards exist because several tiles are depleted (W9=0, T2=0, T6=0, T7=0, T8=0, F1=0, F2=0, J1=0, J2=0, J3=0, etc.).  \n- My exposed meld [T2,T3,T4] reduces flexibility for Chow-heavy hand.  \n- T5 appears twice\u2014a good candidate for a pair, or I can keep if I want a pung.  \n- W3 is triple, a concealed pung possibility, good for scoring if kept.  \n- Opponents\u2019 melds: Next player has Kong of W9, Pung of J1, Chow B5-B7; across has Chow T6-T8; previous has two Chows T6-T8 and T2-T4, plus Chow W6-W8. This suggests many terminals and simple tiles are in play; W tiles (especially W3\u2013W8) may be contested.\n\nConsidering safety:  \n- W5, W4, W3 are relatively safe to discard? Unshown counts: W3:4, W4:4, W5:3. Others have not taken many W tiles.  \n- B3: unshown=3, B6: unshown=3 \u2014 not super dangerous.  \n- T5: unshown=4, but opponents have taken T2-T4, T6-T8 already, so T5 is relatively safe as a middle tile.\n\nBut the hand currently has multiple duplicates: W3\u00d73, T5\u00d72, B3\u00d72, B6\u00d72.  \nBest plan: aim for a hand with two pairs to go for Seven Pairs (24 points), or a standard 4 melds + pair.  \nFor Seven Pairs, I\u2019d need 7 distinct pairs. Currently I have:  \n1. Pair W3? Actually W3 is triple \u2192 counts as 1.5 pairs in Seven Pairs (can be